#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41075c73-ac32-50df-b67a-219517ab45b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336641} {:answer/id #uuid "73b29c73-3eff-5a83-ab61-9d41933b2487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337074} {:answer/id #uuid "8850099e-98d9-52f0-8325-be56acb31a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337261} {:answer/id #uuid "e3364816-8691-5913-b547-c8e857be5fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338013} {:answer/id #uuid "f99cc284-6430-56f2-8e82-685f9498c0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336641} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f5fea84-d7a3-5122-bf16-1ff190f164ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7617fec2-cebf-5dcc-8f77-3696984cb4f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-71532" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=18$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2} &=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2) &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124256 :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124257 :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y) = 6x^2+2y^2 - 2xy^2,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y) = k$ at the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0, \\: y-y_0 \\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (3,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2x{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(12x-2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4y-4xy)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,3) & =(12(3)-2(3)^{2})i+(4(3)-4(3)(3))j \\\\  & =18\\,i-24\\,j \\\\  & =⟨18,-24⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the level curve that passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)=(3,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(3,3)\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨18,-24⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,\\,y-3⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can leave our answer in this form. Or, if we want to leave our final answer in Cartesian form, we expand the dot product and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18(x-3)-24(y-3) & =0 \\\\ 18x-24y+18 & =0 \\\\ 3x-4y+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/102f55fe0add794d3ee0f9f044b5e35c4403d4f160fe36e97d737d33ef3abb68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $6xy+3y^2 =7+ y - 3x^2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{j}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = 6xy+3y^2 - y + 3x^2 ,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=7.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x,y) = f_y(x,y) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x,y) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy+3{y}^{2}-y+3{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $\\mathbf{j},$ we must have $\\mathbf t \\cdot \\mathbf j = 0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((6x+6y-1)\\,i-(6y+6x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -6y-6x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy+3{y}^{2} & =7+y-3{x}^{2} \\\\ 6x(-x)+3(-x)^{2} & =7+(-x)-3{x}^{2} \\\\ -6{x}^{2}+3{x}^{2} & =7-x-3{x}^{2} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if $x=7,$ we get $y=-7$ and the corresponding point is $(7,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :question/id #uuid "b8884af1-186f-582b-8de2-a137a66649b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3432" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124266 :question/id #uuid "3ab5ece1-03aa-5fb0-9952-b858b6ca67b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3433" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,\n$$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124267 :question/id #uuid "d3222782-abc1-5cd3-bceb-27e0585f9e09" :question/math-academy-id "e-3434" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124268 :question/id #uuid "db58088e-59e0-5ca4-82a2-991bb6a4e7be" :question/math-academy-id "e-3435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1-2}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124367 :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239" :question/problem "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c03dc64-0f3b-5e8f-a8ea-480cf8c23bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} {:answer/id #uuid "c892c477-c790-5a52-9139-e23e36c04a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122224} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac772a4-2c3f-5016-95a7-5056a887f722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122225} {:answer/id #uuid "577e0ce2-bfb6-5fd9-aa5b-620334d6829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122226} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6d19bd-365e-5681-9a90-a675402ec3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122227}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186122223} :answer-field/id #uuid "71ff1c87-4a2b-5ff3-8f60-90ba048bfa23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f613246-2fd9-5309-ad22-34ad0114450f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35952" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2668e40a-2488-5d2e-bb1f-0121fc724561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448572} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2c69fe-bead-5476-89ac-9bfc46f40175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448572} :answer-field/id #uuid "034e2028-2f8f-51e2-9def-352dd1c887b9" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449326} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2baa656c-df91-580b-a9c3-4500a888e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448574} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc92910-8986-56a6-8799-16bcee2e1e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448575} :answer-field/id #uuid "d69e547b-615d-50fb-b328-e31355652e5b" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449327} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7b60fef-2a9f-5f32-8282-17b9017027d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448576} {:answer/id #uuid "8cbe814b-8f7e-5508-a800-8e2be0eccd01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448576} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e31b0e0-9526-5208-b591-b3210a53bee0" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6a81953f-1927-53ea-b174-321fab218b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-35953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $1$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a668efeb-9196-55f8-9b55-0356db924def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- (5)/((x - y)^{2})(yi - xj)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122241} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fd1360-8206-5747-923f-e6bf5289202a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- (5)/((x - y)^{2})(xi - yj)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122242} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c956e9-6137-5353-ae04-fcff7a793ac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(5)/((x - y)^{2})(yi + xj)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122243} {:answer/id #uuid "647b8faa-7bab-5d6a-a230-2a682d9c494b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5(xi - yj)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122244} {:answer/id #uuid "6d99f357-3be3-5bf2-92f7-0e5cb85c4117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5(yi - xj)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122245}] :answer-field/id #uuid "62bc8146-116e-5fde-8c73-4e57f913d974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c9824799-8fb2-5e3e-9b52-a3b623c77e1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35988" :question/problem "Given $f(x, y) = \\frac{3x + 2y}{x - y}$, find $∇f(x, y)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "963f66d7-3d07-5c1c-a906-911fb4160ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16i + 12j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122247} {:answer/id #uuid "dedf13c8-6492-5ce9-adb6-02099cac668d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9i - 6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122248} {:answer/id #uuid "ebad106f-c6e1-5bc0-92ca-07b83277ff4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7i + 7j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122249} {:answer/id #uuid "35b9a31f-43c2-5a8b-80f7-40b18eda4e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9i + 12j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122250} {:answer/id #uuid "78b8585a-1fee-5442-ad1f-086c6fec4a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16i + 6j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122251}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8a3693-94d1-5f69-b295-4dd86e337cfd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122246} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346b2e9f-4528-5585-8bdd-4b6c250c3a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\text{ }i+7\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385339} {:answer/id #uuid "1496169a-a34e-5c7c-a33e-01bcc92a83d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\text{ }i+12\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385340} {:answer/id #uuid "4d286a7f-678b-52d6-8ce0-3b5d5647c398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\text{ }i-6\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385341} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e4b564-3de7-5373-898e-ef2bbd58a050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\text{ }i+12\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385342} {:answer/id #uuid "bbde4ba9-e3e1-5bae-a528-0e5a5c8a6786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385343}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385339} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f09fa-dd25-5489-a60d-df7179ee069a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cbddcc6-8b2b-5a15-8d28-6f6aaa082457" :question/math-academy-id "q-35990" :question/problem "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(2,3)$ if $h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =8x-3y+0+0 \\\\  & =8x-3y \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0-3x+4y+1 \\\\  & =4y-3x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & ={h}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(8x-3y)\\text{ }i+(4y-3x+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇h(2,3) & =(8\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)\\text{ }i+(4\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7\\text{ }i+7\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28c62d9-42c2-5bab-af79-c25e2c1af6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6},\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322430} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdf801e-cc4b-53df-a3aa-d41d5909acc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322612} {:answer/id #uuid "ce509177-3d5a-5986-a13b-d30b4334afa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322757} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d388fe-66aa-582e-9f0b-761be7954f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{6},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322972} {:answer/id #uuid "fe13f225-c620-5ad1-9d61-c77c2908a9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323151}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322972} :answer-field/id #uuid "691827ae-2fb8-5708-9d35-1f7788b44561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da858976-fa9b-559a-a71d-31223d9b1ce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-35995" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =6x+0-1 \\\\  & =6x-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x) \\\\  & =0+2y-0 \\\\  & =2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=(6x-1)\\,i+2y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(6x-1)\\,i+2y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x-1 &=0 \\\\ 2y &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\frac{1}{6}.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{6},0)\\,.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc366fb7-3b96-5cce-8c18-91533c332205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379792} {:answer/id #uuid "11d83d6b-c9ac-505b-a024-a94f01ef8b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379793} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1c860c-a8be-5c8e-a6b1-b6df7568e65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379794} {:answer/id #uuid "d13b9873-b619-5955-9c0b-cf1d23785560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379795} {:answer/id #uuid "0437f9db-af07-5f6d-8f9b-9e9b0ad83cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379796} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8afbaeb-57f5-529f-a20d-f5f369208770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c919dc70-fb51-55ac-9d45-4a7c363f49b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-36135" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-2,3)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,3)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,3)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-2)^{2}+(-2)\\cdot {3}^{2}}{(-2)^{2}+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-18}{4-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f8e544-1ea9-599e-974a-71d2d151a87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321442} {:answer/id #uuid "348405b8-ded5-5a35-a88c-d99faa23725d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16x{y}^{3}\\text{ }i+12{x}^{4}y\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321545} {:answer/id #uuid "5942edfe-58e8-5af7-8cfa-2852eccc2bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+({x}^{4}y-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321851} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8e1067-ba7b-54fb-af35-6a7524d7c253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3}+12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322003} {:answer/id #uuid "85783609-ce00-565d-bb5e-3dd88e517710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321442} :answer-field/id #uuid "6498a189-6a46-5093-a0ab-23fe9a246ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7958e50f-73b4-5252-b8f0-e891d3332089" :question/math-academy-id "q-36144" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $g(x,y)h(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x,y) & =3{x}^{2}-4y,\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=6x\\text{ }i-4\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ h(x,y) & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, &  & ∇h(x,y)=4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $g(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[gh]=h\\,∇\\,g+g\\,∇\\,h\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[g(x,y)h(x,y)] & =h(x,y)∇g(x,y)+g(x,y)∇h(x,y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(6x\\text{ }i-4\\text{ }j)+(3{x}^{2}-4y)(4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot 6x\\text{ }i-2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4\\text{ }j)+((3{x}^{2}-4y)\\cdot 4x{y}^{2}\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}-4y)\\cdot 4{x}^{2}y\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\text{ }i-8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\text{ }j+(12{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-16{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(24{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-16x{y}^{3})\\text{ }i+(12{x}^{4}y-24{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93dcc0e-bacf-58c5-b6f4-47f43da168f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122415} {:answer/id #uuid "370200a2-bd3f-5447-b9b1-ea23d53237b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122417} {:answer/id #uuid "42dfcda5-2b9e-563b-b4e1-fb6d44bf17e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122418} {:answer/id #uuid "1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509745} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510220}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186122417} :answer-field/id #uuid "37f65865-99f1-54aa-953e-7c631fab08cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9e917aee-92ff-5d58-8526-d5d5bb6b5240" :question/math-academy-id "q-36311" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cece21e-845c-5406-87a0-7fadb456d6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439641} {:answer/id #uuid "de5a9a5b-9a4a-5dff-ae52-46bed3fefdc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439642} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d13053-52c8-5327-9cdc-96cbda5b9553" :answer-field/key "$(-1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035c9f0e-cadd-5cfc-8ae0-b3b87f593d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439643} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d5e2b8-8c44-503b-8c27-5c384d57c17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439644}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439643} :answer-field/id #uuid "64fa11ce-20a4-54cb-88de-22d21465fcd6" :answer-field/key "$(-1,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440179} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3046be51-904e-5bd4-a31d-cda140ffe757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439645} {:answer/id #uuid "04e55ddc-c7d8-538f-b3d7-db172d2edb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439645} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ebe724a-21ce-5911-9f0e-611acee15d50" :answer-field/key "$(0,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065c6ba1-11bd-5ba4-866d-75daa017cda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439647} {:answer/id #uuid "d416187c-d1f3-5830-8186-2665514f8dbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439648}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439647} :answer-field/id #uuid "08a8c792-30e0-5fbb-bbc3-bd9cc974b2e2" :answer-field/key "$(1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440181} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4ec5074-b1af-55d2-b87e-abf9b7184442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439649} {:answer/id #uuid "223a19d3-0ddf-546c-8353-4b22a48e3261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439650} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b25df16-15af-5c49-a2f0-8d1d38790392" :answer-field/key "$(1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440182}] :question/id #uuid "fa8c1acf-fd5d-5946-ab8b-13077cafbbf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36332" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy + 3y^{2} = x^{2} + 4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve as F(x,y)=0. A tangent is (F_y,-F_x); its dot product with the given vector is $2 x - 2 y$. Solving this with the curve equation gives $(-1,-1), (1,1)$, and the tangent is nonzero at each point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38c70724-aaae-5f8b-90ba-0c075fe647ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321579} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3836ff-c967-550a-b987-849d6e75299d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321892} {:answer/id #uuid "62b7f57c-9049-5269-a2d4-b7a2f5cbdbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} {:answer/id #uuid "9f6b18d1-1602-5fab-90d5-83a3fb2ed5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322404} {:answer/id #uuid "cdecf84e-1100-5475-ac8c-0fad8bf69a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321917} :answer-field/id #uuid "8225f4bf-8597-5194-8000-7f67e378df28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aec682f0-94b9-5c3a-a680-f47e16fecc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-36339" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $6xy=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6xy & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(10x-6y)\\,i+(2y-6x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(10-6(1))\\,i+(2(1)-6)\\,j \\\\  & =4\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,1)=⟨4,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{4} & =\\frac{y-1}{-4} \\\\ x-1 & =-y+1 \\\\ x+y-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e70550-117c-5406-b1d0-1972e33a49a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321186} {:answer/id #uuid "2878fe71-6d72-558f-873e-69c5871866b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321464} {:answer/id #uuid "586da6b9-862c-580b-8d45-8410265668b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbac82-4e87-5e31-9d1f-ff934afb295a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321999} {:answer/id #uuid "edc7b79a-bbdb-5b22-bcb8-45d937f420aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321841} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0fe214-7371-518b-8883-59f70ef20780" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "967a9051-3e85-5612-827f-8db0c0985ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36343" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ at the point $(2,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{2}\\text{ }i-2y\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,0,1) & =\\frac{(2)}{2}\\,i-2(0)\\text{ }j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =i-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(2,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(2,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(2,0,1)=⟨2,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,i-12\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509724} {:answer/id #uuid "3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,i+7\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509819} {:answer/id #uuid "812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-4j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509971} {:answer/id #uuid "91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509997} {:answer/id #uuid "cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33a5905-3216-54dd-ad86-072ac416bdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e2999c4-1892-5dd4-a533-5ed9b93e52c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-36378" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3567d85d-c8e4-5c8e-a5fa-9a03f4e73154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨6,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447336} {:answer/id #uuid "81b0821b-7e46-5c33-9092-7826606b26db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447339} {:answer/id #uuid "aad3ab95-80b7-58a9-8f7a-b59b2f76d6e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-4,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447343} {:answer/id #uuid "b3e86513-1b68-561a-a8d3-dbdeb9989260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447344} {:answer/id #uuid "dad53699-365d-5be5-97a0-c5dd9eb33c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447343} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbade43c-aee7-5def-a760-a6555c7e377c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ecf0fad-2e50-567b-a7b0-a4806d471317" :question/math-academy-id "q-36383" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+2x{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(6x+2{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4xy-1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇\\,f(-1,1) & =(6(-1)+2(1)^{2})\\,i+(4(-1)(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-4\\,i-5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-4,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3021435f-4fc5-5325-909c-5c4aa929c4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458723} {:answer/id #uuid "a73148e2-c414-5427-b687-2d5af8547b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458927} {:answer/id #uuid "b0875f94-b02d-58ee-876a-7de7153c2d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458945} {:answer/id #uuid "c99e77dc-e78f-5088-831c-2eddf2080e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186458985} {:answer/id #uuid "fec8b574-6fb0-5eaa-9cb4-748309ad20fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459081} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea33b86b-0147-5727-9ec2-461d7ee2b6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d538882c-b1cd-5192-bbe8-86de583ea5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-36391" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y=xy+4.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =xy+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-xy & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,2)={f}_{y}(2,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-x)\\,i-(2xy-y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,2) & =({2}^{2}-2)\\,i-(2(2)(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-6\\,j.$"}]}
